    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Mongolian is the principal Mongolic language; its written history begins in the 13th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> adopted a script from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> spoke Middle Mongol.</li><li>Its vowels follow strict harmony and its grammar builds words from chains of suffixes, with seven to nine cases and five voices.</li><li>Written in more scripts than almost any other language, it is spoken today by perhaps 5–6 million people, in Cyrillic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and in the traditional script in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, where since 2023 Mandarin has been the only language of instruction in its schools.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Cyrillic script, closer to the spoken language than the traditional one, helped raise literacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> from 17.3% to 73.5% between 1941 and 1950; campaigns using the traditional script had raised it only from 3.0% to 17.3% between 1921 and 1940.</p></aside>
    </section>

